# Code Change Principles

Apply to all code generation, refactors, and tests.

## Architecture & Module Boundaries

- Follow the existing 4-layer structure (`core_engine`, `cryptography`, `network`, `storage`) and module ownership described in [architecture-guide.mdc](mdc:.cursor/rules/architecture-guide.mdc) and [docs/architecture.md](mdc:docs/architecture.md).
- Keep domain types in their owning modules (e.g. shared types in `src/common/`); do not redefine the same domain type in multiple layers.
- Keep composition-root responsibilities in [src/main.nim](mdc:src/main.nim): wire modules there; do not scatter orchestration into leaf modules.
- Prefer a single canonical state/API surface per concern; do not introduce parallel state APIs that overlap existing ones.
- Prefer qualified or explicitly disambiguated imports over ambiguous unqualified imports that collide on type or procedure names.

## Change Discipline

- Prefer small, incremental, reviewable changes over broad rewrites.
- Preserve existing public behavior unless the user explicitly requests a breaking or behavioral change.
- Extend existing patterns before inventing new ones.

## Language

- Write all code comments, documentation, ADRs, and commit messages in English.
